  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/5db4386a-b0a0-41b9-b795-d6a81ff39b1a-9894481.PNG\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 238px;\" /></p><p>The expression of motional emf is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></math>, where,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#952;</mi></math>&nbsp;is angle between magnetic field and direction of motion.</p><p>Here,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>,&nbsp;</p><p>So, induced emf across the ends of wire is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>sin</mi><mn>90</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>v</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math></p>",
